BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Romeo + Juliet in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260904T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260904T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc89c-0b7d-e148-2d01-91e901f619ab@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, September 4\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h\n\n1996 | 120 min. | Australia/USA/
 Canada | Color | Scope | English | Rated PG-13 | 35mm  \nDIRECTED BY: Baz
  Luhrmann  \nWRITTEN BY: Craig Pearce\, Baz Luhrmann  \nPRODUCED BY: Gab
 riella Martinelli\, Baz Luhrmann  \nWITH: Leonardo DiCaprio\, Claire Dane
 s\, Brian Dennehy\, John Leguizamo\nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Arc
 hive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nBaz Luhrmann’s take on Shakespeare’s trag
 edy is perhaps one of the best-loved adaptations and is the second install
 ment in his Red Curtain Trilogy\, which closed with Moulin Rouge! (2001). 
 Featuring Luhrmann’s distinctive\, stylized palate coupled with frenetic
 \, MTV-style editing and an all-star soundtrack\, Romeo + Juliet became a 
 classic for its casting\, with Leonardo DiCaprio and Claire Danes playing 
 the star-crossed lovers.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/romeo-juliet-019fc89c
 -0b7d-e148-2d01-91e901f619ab
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260905T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260905T121500
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8af-40aa-8422-5027-ead44125da7f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, September 5\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 23m\n\n1937 | 83 min. | USA | Color
  | English | Rated G | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: David Hand\, Perce Pearce\, Will
 iam Cottrell\, Larry Morey\, Wilfred Jackson\, Ben Sharpsteen \nPRODUCED 
 BY: Walt Disney  \nWRITTEN BY: Ted Sears\, Richard Creedon\, Otto England
 er\, Dick Rickard\, Earl Hurd\, Merrill De Maris\, Dorothy Ann Blank\, Web
 b Smith \nWITH: Adriana Caselotti\, Harry Stockwell\, Lucille La Verne\, 
 Moroni Olsen\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSnow White\, orphaned as a child\, is 
 forced to work under the reign of her evil stepmother\, the Queen\, who wi
 ll stop at nothing to remain the most beautiful being in the land. When Sn
 ow White’s beauty grows\, so does the Queen’s jealously. Seething with
  hatred\, the Queen concocts a sinister plan to put an end to her. Will th
 e princess escape her fate and achieve her happily ever after? Based on th
 e classic Brothers Grimm fairy tale\, this first animated feature from The
  Walt Disney Company—and the first traditionally animated feature ever p
 roduced— was nominated for its original score\; the following year\, the
  Academy gave Disney a Special Award for the film (one full-sized Oscar an
 d seven small ones)\, “recognized as a significant screen innovation whi
 ch has charmed millions and pioneered a great new entertainment field for 
 the motion picture cartoon.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/snow-white-and-the-se
 ven-dwarfs-019fc8af-40aa-8422-5027-ead44125da7f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fanny & Alexander in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260905T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260905T163000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc89d-9071-0842-2700-77f8e4ef116e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, September 5\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy 
 Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 3h 8m\n\n1982 | 188 min. | Sweden/Fr
 ance/West Germany | Color | Swedish/German/Yiddish with English subtitles 
 | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Ingmar Bergman \nPRODUCED BY: Jör
 n Donner \nWITH: Kristina Adolphson\, Börje Ahlstedt\, Bertil Guve\, Per
 nilla Allwin\nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Scr
 eenings\n\nSwedish auteur Ingmar Bergman’s most sentimental and autobiog
 raphical work is set in turn-of-the-century Sweden and appropriately bathe
 d in the candlelit interiors common of the era. Unfolding through the eyes
  of 10-year-old Alexander\, a boy from a wealthy family that operates the 
 town theater\, the narrative spans seasons and holidays\, marriages and de
 aths\, with small moments of daily life punctuating childhood milestones. 
 Originally conceived as a television miniseries of more than five hours\, 
 the final\, 188-minute version allowed the visuals of two-time Oscar-winni
 ng cinematographer Sven Nykvist (Fanny & Alexander was one of his wins) to
  be admired at a scale befitting this epic drama.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/fanny-alexander-019fc
 89d-9071-0842-2700-77f8e4ef116e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Top Hat in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260906T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260906T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8b4-d768-678d-0db9-a00bf429e330@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, September 6\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 40m\n\n1935 | 100 min. | USA | Black-a
 nd-White | English | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Mark Sandrich \nWRITTEN BY: Dwigh
 t Taylor\, Allan Scott \nSTORY BY: Dwight Taylor \nPRODUCED BY: Pandro S. 
 Berman \nWITH: Fred Astaire\, Ginger Rogers\, Edward Everett Horton\, Erik
  Rhodes \nNew print courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screeni
 ngs\n\nAn American tap dancer pursues the woman he loves from London to Ve
 nice in the fourth movie musical to feature the iconic team of Fred Astair
 e and Ginger Rogers\, and the second of their films to receive a Best Pict
 ure nomination\, following The Gay Divorcee (1934). Spotlighting the stars
  at their effortless best\, it was also nominated for Irving Berlin’s cl
 assic “Cheek to Cheek\,” Hermes Pan’s dance direction of the numbers
  “Piccolino” and “Top Hat\, White Tie\, and Tails\,” and the art d
 irection of Van Nest Polglase and Carroll Clark\, whose elegant deco sets 
 include a Venice canal constructed on a Los Angeles soundstage.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/top-hat-019fc8b4-d768
 -678d-0db9-a00bf429e330
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Amadeus in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260906T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260906T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc89e-7904-149f-2c28-cb7ff0e1a99a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, September 6\, 2026 at 6 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 38m\n\n1984 | 158 min | USA | Color | 
 Scope | English | Rated PG | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Miloš Forman  \nPRODUC
 ED BY: Saul Zaentz  \nWRITTEN BY: Peter Shaffer  \nWITH: F. Murray Abrah
 am\, Tom Hulce\, Elizabeth Berridge\, Simon Callow\nOriginal theatrical re
 lease version 4k restoration by The Academy Film Archive and The Saul Zaen
 tz Co. with restoration funding provided by Teatro Della Pace Films.\n\nTy
 pe: Screenings\n\nMiloš Forman directed Peter Shaffer’s screenplay\, ba
 sed on his Tony Award–winning play\, about the 18th century rivalry betw
 een journeyman court composer Antonio Salieri (F. Murray Abraham in an Aca
 demy Award–winning tour de force) and young upstart Wolfgang Amadeus Moz
 art (fellow Best Actor nominee Tom Hulce). Saul Zaentz’s lavish producti
 on features opulent period details\, while Shaffer’s witty script speaks
  eloquently about the challenge of art while leaving room for Mozart’s g
 orgeous music. The film earned 11 nominations\, and its eight Oscar wins i
 nclude Best Picture\, Art Direction\, Costume Design\, Directing\, Makeup
 \, Sound\, and for Shaffer’s adapted screenplay.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/amadeus-019fc89e-7904
 -149f-2c28-cb7ff0e1a99a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Niagara
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260907T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260907T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc891-49c1-51ed-a07d-1b35ecb4a43a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, September 7\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 32m\n\n1953 | 92 min | USA | Techni
 color | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Henry Hathaway \nPRODUCED
  BY: Charles Brackett \nWRITTEN BY: Charles Brackett\, Walter Reisch\, Ric
 hard Breen \nWITH: Marilyn Monroe\, Joseph Cotten\, Jean Peters\, Casey Ad
 ams\n\nType: Screenings\n\nNewlyweds Ray and Polly Cutler (Jean Peters and
  Casey Adams) take a postponed honeymoon at Niagara Falls\, where they enc
 ounter Rose and George Loomis (Marilyn Monroe and Joseph Cotten). The Loom
 is’s rocky relationship hangs over the couple’s romantic getaway\, fur
 ther complicated by a murder. 1953 was an important year for Monroe with t
 he successes of Gentlemen Prefer Blondes and How to Marry a Millionaire in
  the following months\, and her breakout performance as the sensual Rose i
 n Niagara catapulted the actor into superstardom.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/niagara-019fc891-49c1
 -51ed-a07d-1b35ecb4a43a
END:VEVENT
