BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Beguiled
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260911T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260911T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc89f-31e0-c442-3bb6-f8da32fc5b15@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, September 11\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 33m\n\n2017 | 93 min | USA | Color
  | English | Rated R \nDIRECTED BY: Sofia Coppola \nPRODUCED BY: Youree He
 nley\, Sofia Coppola \nWRITTEN BY: Sofia Coppola \nWITH: Colin Farrell\, N
 icole Kidman\, Kirsten Dunst\, Elle Fanning\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA stude
 nt from a Virginian girl's school during the American Civil War discovers 
 a Union Army corporal wounded in the woods and takes him in. Attracted to 
 the handsome enemy soldier\, the women of the home allow him to stay. This
  intense gothic thriller is filled with jealousy\, rage\, and betrayals. B
 ased on the 1966 novel by Thomas P. Cullinan\, this adaptation stars Colin
  Farrell\, Nicole Kidman\, Elle Fanning\, and Kirsten Dunst. The Beguiled 
 earned director Sofia Coppola the Prix de la mise en scene (Best Director 
 Award) at the 2017 Cannes Film Festival.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-beguiled-019fc89f
 -31e0-c442-3bb6-f8da32fc5b15
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Monster Squad in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260912T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260912T121500
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8af-c7c1-cc40-dcf5-37d5f34ff0cc@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, September 12\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 22m\n\n1987 | 82 min. | USA | Colo
 r | Scope | English | Rated PG-13 | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Fred Dekker \nPRO
 DUCED BY: Jonathan A. Zimbert \nWRITTEN BY: Shane Black\, Fred Dekker \n
 WITH: Andre Gower\, Robby Kiger\, Stephen Macht\, Duncan Regehr \nPrint co
 urtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSean Crenshaw (
 Andre Gower) is the leader of his school's monster club\, a group of monst
 er- and horror film-loving pre-teens who get the thrill of their lives whe
 n their favorite on-screen creatures show up in their own backyard. As Dra
 cula\, Wolf-Man\, Gill-Man\, and the Mummy join forces to steal a magical 
 amulet and take over the world\, it is up to these plucky kids to use thei
 r knowledge of horror tropes to stop them. The movie struggled to find an 
 audience\, thanks in part to its newly created PG-13 rating\, but it has a
 chieved cult status among horror fans\, with many citing it as the film th
 at sparked their love for genre as children.\nPlease note: This film conta
 ins the use of homophobic language.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-monster-squad-019
 fc8af-c7c1-cc40-dcf5-37d5f34ff0cc
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Portrait of a Lady on Fire
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260912T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260912T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8a0-d524-8cb2-051d-d09e1e5c7580@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, September 12\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy
  Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 2m\n\n2019 | 122 min | France | 
 Color | French\, Italian with English subtitles | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRE
 CTED/WRITTEN BY: Céline Sciamma \nPRODUCED BY: Bénédicte Couvreur \nW
 ITH: Noémie Merlant\, Adèle Haenel\, Luàna Bajrami\, Valeria Golino\n\n
 Type: Screenings\n\nThe first film directed by a woman to receive the Quee
 r Palm at the Cannes Film Festival\, Céline Sciamma’s 18th-century roma
 nce revels in the sensuous affair between aristocrat Héloïse (Adèle Hae
 nel) and artist Marianne (Noémie Merlant)\, who is commissioned to paint 
 her portrait. Though betrothed to a wealthy man of the Milanese upper clas
 s\, the stubborn Héloïse refuses to be captured\, either by her male sui
 tor or the artist meant to paint her. Sciamma’s tale of sexual liberatio
 n finds beauty in the electric connection between her leading women for a 
 gorgeous and aching story of forbidden love and the fleeting nature of rom
 ance.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/portrait-of-a-lady-on
 -fire-019fc8a0-d524-8cb2-051d-d09e1e5c7580
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Black Narcissus
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260913T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260913T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8b6-0074-37d8-71f0-06229547163d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, September 13\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 41m\n\n1947 | 101 min. | UK | Color |
  English | DCP \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN/PRODUCED BY: Michael Powell\, Emeric Pre
 ssburger \nWITH: Deborah Kerr\, Sabu\, David Farrar\, Flora Robson\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\nSister Clodagh (Deborah Kerr) enlists others from her nunn
 ery to found a school and hospital in the Himalayan palace of Mopu\, where
  the unrelenting winds and extraordinary beauty of the landscape distract 
 the sisters from their mission. With hues so vibrant the images seem to br
 eathe\, Jack Cardiff\, who won his first Oscar for Cinematography (Color) 
 on this project\, pushes Technicolor to its limits. He’s supported by th
 e striking\, Oscar-winning art direction of frequent Michael Powell-Emeric
  Pressburger collaborator Alfred Junge in what Powell has called “the mo
 st erotic film I have ever made.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/black-narcissus-019fc
 8b6-0074-37d8-71f0-06229547163d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Making Marie Antoinette Book Signing with Sofia Coppola
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260913T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260913T180000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fdd99-9e6f-bd6d-2361-838c573abcca@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Book Signings
DESCRIPTION:Mark the release of Making Marie Antoinette with Academy Award
 -winning writer and director Sofia Coppola at the Academy Museum.\n\nSunda
 y\, September 13\, 2026 at 4 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pict
 ures\n\nType: Book Signings\n\nMark the release of Making Marie Antoinette
  with Academy Award-winning writer and director Sofia Coppola at the Acade
 my Museum.\nWhen Coppola set out to make her ambitious third feature film
 \, she was given unprecedented access to the Palace of Versailles\, where 
 she created a world whose influence continues to reverberate nearly 20 yea
 rs later. There to document it all was Coppola's longtime friend and colla
 borator Andrew Durham\, who photographed daily life on and off the set fro
 m production through the film's premiere at Cannes. Making Marie Antoinett
 e brings together an unprecedented collection of Durham's photos\, alongsi
 de an oral history of the film's making by Keaton Bell and a new conversat
 ion between Durham and Coppola\, offering a rare look into the making and 
 mythology of one of Coppola's most celebrated films.\nEach ticket includes
  one copy of Making Marie Antoinette\, which Sofia Coppola signs during th
 e event.\nGuests may also purchase a separate ticket to a special presenta
 tion of Marie Antoinette (2006)\, beginning with a conversation with Coppo
 la\, followed by a screening of this sumptuous feature.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/making-marie-antoinet
 te-book-signing-with-sofia-coppola-019fdd99-9e6f-bd6d-2361-838c573abcca
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:20th Anniversary of Marie Antoinette with Sofia Coppola
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260913T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260913T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8ba-df50-d2c5-e7fd-83e0160ed0fa@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: director Sofia Coppola and actor Kirsten Dunst\n\nS
 unday\, September 13\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 3m\n\n2006 | 123 min | USA\, France\, Japan | C
 olor | English\, Latin\, French | Rated PG-13 | 4K DCP  \nDIRECTED/WRITTE
 N BY: Sofia Coppola \nPRODUCED BY: Sofia Coppola\, Ross Katz\nWITH: Kirste
 n Dunst\, Jason Schwartzman\, Steve Coogan\, Judy Davis\n\nType: Screening
 s\n\nThe third feature film from Oscar-winning writer-director Sofia Coppo
 la (Lost in Translation\, 2003) transposes her fascination with the inner 
 lives of young women to 18th century Versailles with this semi-biographica
 l story of Marie Antoinette\, Queen of France from 1774 to 1793. Inspired 
 by her favorite period dramas and biopics\, including Ken Russell’s Lisz
 tomania (1975) and Milos Forman’s Amadeus (1984)\, Coppola gained extrao
 rdinary access to shoot at the Palace at Versailles\, and her attention to
  production design and costuming details cushion her characters in lavish
 \, insulated surroundings—and garnered the film’s costume designer Mil
 ena Canonero her third Oscar win. Punctuated with anachronistic postpunk a
 nd new wave music\, Coppola’s refreshing take on a centuries-old history
  brings the woes of the past into sharp comparison with present-day concer
 ns of girls\, teens\, and young women for a deeply felt and beautifully re
 ndered cinematic achievement. \nIn a separately ticketed event before the 
 screening\, Sofia Coppola signs copies of her new book\, Making Marie Anto
 inette\, at the Academy Museum Store.  \nProgrammed and note by K.J. Relt
 h-Miller.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/20th-anniversary-of-m
 arie-antoinette-with-sofia-coppola-and-019fc8ba-df50-d2c5-e7fd-83e0160ed0f
 a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gentlemen Prefer Blondes
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260916T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260916T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc893-5ef9-6556-1b7c-6a48b8cf70ac@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, September 16\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academ
 y Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 31m\n\n1953 | 91 min. | USA | T
 echnicolor | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Howard Hawks \nPROD
 UCED BY: Sol C. Siegel \nWRITTEN BY: Charles Lederer \nWITH: Jane Russell
 \, Marilyn Monroe\, Charles Coburn\, Elliott Reid\n\nType: Screenings\n\nI
 n this 1953 musical comedy\, adapted from Joseph Fields and Anita Loos’s
  searingly funny stage musical based on her 1925 novella\, showgirl Lorele
 i (Marilyn Monroe) is resplendent in beautiful gowns\, most notably in the
  pink satin dress designed by Travilla during the now-classic number “Di
 amonds Are a Girl’s Best Friend.” Lorelei is seen throughout the pictu
 re in jewel tones and literal jewels\, carefully selected to play up the l
 ush Technicolor cinematography lensed by Harry J. Wild. Both effervescent 
 and timeless\, this witty gem showcases Monroe’s brilliant comic timing 
 as well as her vocal chops. Premiering in the middle of the actress’s ca
 reer and released the same year as Niagara and How to Marry a Millionaire
 \, Howard Hawks’s film helped to plant Monroe in the hearts of the Ameri
 can public\, making her one of the most famous people in the US.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/gentlemen-prefer-blon
 des-019fc893-5ef9-6556-1b7c-6a48b8cf70ac
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Emma in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260916T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260916T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8aa-b2f6-34e4-0af9-8a7dffc5f782@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Music Branch \nFree for museum members\n\nWedn
 esday\, September 16\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Moti
 on Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h\n\n1996 | 120 min. | USA | Color | English | Ra
 ted PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Douglas McGrath \nPRODUCED BY: Steven
  Haft\, Patrick Cassavetti \nWITH: Gwyneth Paltrow\, Toni Collette\, Alan 
 Cumming\, Ewan McGregor\nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nTy
 pe: Screenings\n\nRachel Portman was the first woman to win an Academy Awa
 rd for Music (Original Musical or Comedy Score) for Emma. An adaptation of
  Jane Austen’s novel\, this romantic comedy stars Gwyneth Paltrow as the
  meddling but well-meaning heroine\, whose matchmaking aims cause confusio
 n and heartache in her circles. The film was also nominated for Ruth Myers
 ’s costumes\, and Portman would go on to be nominated two more times for
  her scores for The Cider House Rules (1999) and Chocolat (2000).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/emma-019fc8aa-b2f6-34
 e4-0af9-8a7dffc5f782
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Community Conversation: Marilyn Monroe and Cultural Icons
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T130000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019ff713-a119-88e4-feac-6f06d0320a22@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Mariana Da Silva of El Cine and KC Fralick of Queer Film LA ex
 plore how communities shape the legacies of Hollywood icons such as Marily
 n Monroe.\n\nFriday\, September 18\, 2026 at 12 pm\n\nLocation: Sidney Poi
 tier Grand Lobby\n\nType: Conversations\n\nEl Cine Executive Director Mari
 ana Da Silva and Queer Film LA Manager of Community Engagement KC Fralick 
 explore how different cultural communities shape the legacy of Hollywood i
 dols in a fascinating conversation inspired by the Academy Museum exhibiti
 on Marilyn Monroe: Hollywood Icon. Our guests discuss this engagement in t
 he context of classic Hollywood\; the evolving meaning of movie stardom\; 
 and more.\nSophia Serrano\, Academy Museum of Motion Pictures Associate Cu
 rator\, moderates.
LOCATION:Sidney Poitier Grand Lobby
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/community-conversatio
 n-marilyn-monroe-and-cultural-icons-019ff713-a119-88e4-feac-6f06d0320a22
