BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:50 Movie Nights Book Signing with Dave Karger
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T190000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fdd95-2f6b-38dd-09e7-8f1f80f8f1c6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Book Signings,Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Celebrate the release of 50 Movie Nights with Turner Classic M
 ovies host\, film expert\, and bestselling author Dave Karger at the Acade
 my Museum.\n\nFriday\, September 18\, 2026 at 5:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy
  Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nType: Book Signings\, Conversations\n\nCeleb
 rate the release of 50 Movie Nights with Turner Classic Movies host\, film
  expert\, and bestselling author Dave Karger at the Academy Museum.\nIn 50
  Movie Nights\, Karger brings together 50 engaging conversations with accl
 aimed artists from across the entertainment industry—including Scarlett 
 Johansson\, Paul Rudd\, Sarah Jessica Parker\, Sterling K. Brown\, Carol B
 urnett\, Ava DuVernay\, Don Cheadle\, and many others—who each share the
  20th-century film that has had the greatest impact on their lives. Filled
  with insightful stories\, film recommendations\, and stunning photography
 \, 50 Movie Nights is an invitation to discover—or rediscover—some of 
 the most beloved films ever made.\nEach ticket includes a copy of 50 Movie
  Nights\, signed by Karger during the event.\nGuests may also purchase a s
 eparate ticket to a special presentation of The King of Comedy (1982)\, be
 ginning with a conversation between Karger and actor\, director\, and prod
 ucer Jason Bateman\, exploring the film's enduring legacy\, followed by a 
 screening of Martin Scorsese's comedy.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/50-movie-nights-book-
 signing-with-dave-karger-019fdd95-2f6b-38dd-09e7-8f1f80f8f1c6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The King of Comedy with Dave Karger and Jason Bateman
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8bb-8c3d-b21c-0477-0aafd9055dc2@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Turner Classic Movies host Dave Karger and actor Ja
 son Bateman\n\nFriday\, September 18\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academ
 y Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1982 | 109 min. | USA | 
 Color | English | Rated PG | 4K DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: Martin Scorsese \nPR
 ODUCED BY: Arnon Milchan  \nWRITTEN BY: Paul D. Zimmerman  \nWITH: Rober
 t De Niro\, Jerry Lewis\, Diahnne Abbott\, Sandra Bernhard\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nFollowing their collaborations on Taxi Driver (1976)\, Raging Bull
  (1980)\, and others\, two-time Oscar-winning actor Robert De Niro and Osc
 ar-winning director Martin Scorsese re-teamed for this dark comedy. Zealou
 s aspiring comic Rupert Pupkin collaborates with an obsessive fangirl (San
 dra Bernhard) to kidnap their favorite late-night host (Jerry Lewis). A cr
 itical darling that was a box office failure upon its release\, the film h
 as since become a pop-culture touchstone\, thanks to social media and its 
 empty promises of celebrity access. Actor Jason Bateman has called this on
 e of his favorite films\; he joins Turner Classic Movies host Dave Karger 
 for a conversation about it before the screening.  \nIn a separately tick
 eted event before the conversation\, Karger signs copies of his new book\,
  50 Movie Nights: Contemporary Stars on Their Favorite Films\, at the Acad
 emy Museum Store.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/king-of-comedy-019fc8
 bb-8c3d-b21c-0477-0aafd9055dc2
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Uncles in 16mm with Tacita Dean and Laurence Kardish
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260918T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8a3-dfee-4938-2a9a-b4e894a6e34d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: filmmaker Tacita Dean\, historian Laurence Kardish
 \n\nFriday\, September 18\, 2026 at 8 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Mo
 tion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 17m\n\n2004 | 77 minutes | UK | Color | Engli
 sh | Not Rated | 16mm \nDIRECTED BY: Tacita Dean \nWITH: Winton Dean\, Jon
 athan Balcon\, Laurence Kardish\, Stephen Dean \nPrint courtesy of Marian 
 Goodman Gallery and the Tacita Dean Collection at the Academy Film Archive
 .\n\nType: Screenings\n\nInternationally renowned fine artist Tacita Dean 
 presents her rarely screened The Uncles (2004)\, in which the sons of Eali
 ng Studios icons Basil Dean and Sir Michael Balcon reflect on their father
 s’ careers and personal lives in an extended conversation with historian
  and curator Laurence Kardish. \nFilmed and projected in anamorphic 16mm\,
  The Uncles offers an unusual widescreen portrait of two loquacious\, char
 ming English gentlemen (Winton Dean and Jonathan Balcon) illuminating the 
 accomplishments\, failings\, and elusive humanity of their eminent fathers
  with wit\, candor\, and understated complexity. Beyond the copious anecdo
 tal nuggets of interest to any fan of classic British cinema\, the film al
 so grants intimate access to two men who both admired and struggled to tru
 ly know their fathers as they consider their own youths in pre-war Britain
 . \nIn conjunction with this screening and organized by Los Angeles’ Mar
 ciano Art Foundation (MAF) director Hanneke Skerath and Mark Toscano\, MAF
  will present the US debut of Dean’s landmark installation\, FILM (2011)
 \, from September 18\, 2026\, through January 2\, 2027. \nProgram and note
 s by Academy Film Archive Senior Film Preservationist Mark Toscano.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-uncles-in-16-mm-w
 ith-tacita-dean-019fc8a3-dfee-4938-2a9a-b4e894a6e34d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Something Wicked This Way Comes in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260919T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260919T121500
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8b3-917c-69c6-df9f-edd6613947c8@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, September 19\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1983 | 95 min. | USA | Colo
 r | English | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Jack Clayton \nPRODUCED BY:
  Peter Vincent Douglas \nWRITTEN BY: Ray Bradbury \nWITH: Vidal Peterson
 \, Shawn Carson\, Jason Robards\, Jonathan Pryce\nPrint provided by The Wa
 lt Disney Company.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nIn a small\, boring town\, best 
 friends Jim and Will eagerly await the excitement of the yearly carnival. 
 But after a different festival\, helmed by the sinister Mr. Dark\, unexpec
 tedly sets up overnight\, the town adults begin to fall under his spell\, 
 and the funfair reveals itself as a carnival of terror. Disney funded the 
 project\, based on Ray Bradbury’s short story “Black Ferris\,” in ho
 pes of breaking free from its stereotype of animated family films\; ultima
 tely\, there were extensive edits and reshoots to create a more family-fri
 endly film without losing too many of the thrills.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/something-wicked-this
 -way-comes-019fc8b3-917c-69c6-df9f-edd6613947c8
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Inside Screenwriting: LA Plays Itself with Larry Karaszewski
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260919T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260919T150000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fedd0-08c5-7a04-b5a5-6d6c290ab634@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Academy member Larry Karaszewski talks about his life as a Hol
 lywood scribe in our Screenwriting: Takes on LA Noir gallery.\n\nSaturday
 \, September 19\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Level 2\n\nCost: Free with gen
 eral admission\n\nType: Conversations\n\nAcademy member Larry Karaszewski 
 talks about his life as a Hollywood scribe in our Screenwriting: Takes on 
 LA Noir gallery. He also delves into the many ways film scripts have tried
  to define Los Angeles\, using examples from his own work and the projects
  in the gallery. \nKaraszewski stays for a brief Q&A after the talk.
LOCATION:Level 2
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/inside-screenwriting-
 la-plays-itself-with-larry-karazsewski-019fedd0-08c5-7a04-b5a5-6d6c290ab63
 4
