SUMMARY:Gigi in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260920T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260920T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc8b6-7cf6-5a12-0544-6c813ed43ef3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, September 20\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 56m\n\n1958 | 116 min. | USA | Color 
 | Scope | English | Rated G | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Vincente Minnelli \nWRIT
 TEN BY: Alan Jay Lerner \nPRODUCED BY: Arthur Freed \nWITH: Leslie Caron\,
  Maurice Chevalier\, Louis Jourdan\, Hermione Gingold \nNew print courtesy
  of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis lavish\, widescr
 een musical from director Vincente Minnelli received nine Oscar nomination
 s and won in each of its categories\, including Best Picture and Directing
 . Minnelli’s An American in Paris (1951) discovery Leslie Caron plays Gi
 gi\, a young woman in turn-of-the-20th-century Paris who catches the eye o
 f a charming womanizer (Louis Jourdan). The Oscar-winning screenplay by Al
 an Jay Lerner was based on the 1944 novella by Colette\, and Lerner wrote 
 the film’s original songs with his My Fair Lady (1964) composer Frederic
 k Loewe\, their score including such classics as “I Remember It Well\,
 ” “The Night They Invented Champagne\,” and the Oscar-winning title 
 song. Art directors William A. Horning and Preston Ames and set decorators
  Henry Grace and Keogh Gleason won Oscars for their eye-poppingly colorful
  recreation of the City of Light.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/gigi-019fc8b6-7cf6-5a
 12-0544-6c813ed43ef3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How to Marry a Millionaire
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260921T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260921T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fc894-7141-e330-b73d-4a62f069bd8e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, September 21\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1953 | 95 min | USA | Techn
 icolor | Scope | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Jean Negulesco 
 \nPRODUCED BY: Nunnally Johnson \nWRITTEN BY: Nunnally Johnson \nWITH: Bet
 ty Grable\, Marilyn Monroe\, Lauren Bacall\, David Wayne\n\nType: Screenin
 gs\n\nSchatze\, Pola\, and Loco (Lauren Bacall\, Marilyn Monroe\, Betty Gr
 able) are Manhattan models on a mission to marry rich husbands. Although t
 hey set their sights on Vanderbilts and Rockefellers\, their plans go awry
  when they become attracted to men who look to be penniless. How to Marry 
 a Millionaire was released at the end of Monroe’s breakout year\, having
  starred in Gentlemen Prefer Blondes and Niagara in 1953. The film is nota
 ble as the first to be completed in CinemaScope\, and Monroe had such broa
 d appeal that 20th Century-Fox used her to advertise this new widescreen f
 ormat: a slogan above an image of Marilyn claimed she was “Big as life a
 nd a million times more fun in Cinemascope!”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/how-to-marry-a-millio
 naire-019fc894-7141-e330-b73d-4a62f069bd8e
END:VEVENT
