BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Escape From New York in 4K and Escape from LA presented by John Ca
 rpenter and Kurt Russell
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261003T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261003T213000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcda3-39cc-7bc2-d01c-a3e6ec18e6e9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, October 3\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 39m\n\nDIRECTED BY: John Carpenter 
 \nPRODUCED BY: Larry Franco\, Debra Hill \nWRITTEN BY: John Carpenter\, Ni
 ck Castle \nWITH: Kurt Russell\, Lee Van Cleef\, Ernest Borgnine\, Donald 
 Pleasence \n1981 | 99 min | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated R | 4K D
 CP\n\nType: Screenings\n\nEscape from New York in 4KSet in a grim\, not-to
 o-distant future where a walled-off Manhattan Island has been converted to
  a massive prison\, John Carpenter’s Escape from New York confronts issu
 es of social unrest\, government surveillance and corruption. Kurt Russell
  plays Snake Plissken\, a former bank robber and classic anti-hero\, promi
 sed a pardon if he rescues the American president\, who is trapped in Manh
 attan. The now-iconic character allowed Russell to shed his image as a Dis
 ney live-action leading man and kindled a long collaborative relationship 
 with Carpenter. An early example of the cyberpunk genre in cinema\, the fi
 lm was a critical and commercial success\, influencing many screenwriters 
 and authors.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/escape-from-new-york-
 with-escape-from-la-presented-by-john-carpenter-kurt-russell-019fcda3-39cc
 -7bc2-d01c-a3e6ec18e6e9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cabaret
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261004T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261004T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce45-9a13-42b2-5717-e48163a64c67@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, October 4\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum 
 of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 4m\n\n1972 | 124 min. | USA | Color | En
 glish | Rated PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Bob Fosse \nWRITTEN BY: Jay Allen \n
 PRODUCED BY: Cy Feuer \nWITH: Liza Minnelli\, Michael York\, Helmut Griem
 \, Joel Grey\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe second feature from director-chore
 ographer Bob Fosse is one of his greatest works in any medium. It’s a da
 zzling adaptation of the John Kander-Fred Ebb Broadway show\, inspired by 
 Christopher Isherwood’s The Berlin Stories\, and a fine demonstration of
  how a movie musical rooted in realism can be as satisfying as the more tr
 aditional variety. When naïve young Brian Roberts (Michael York as the Is
 herwood figure) moves into a Berlin boardinghouse in 1931\, he is unprepar
 ed for his new neighbor—the brash\, would-be star Sally Bowles\, played 
 by Liza Minnelli in her Oscar-winning\, career-defining performance. The f
 ilm received 10 nominations (including Best Picture) and won eight Oscars
 \, including for Fosse’s direction\, Joel Grey’s supporting performanc
 e\, and the art direction of Rolf Zehetbauer\, Jurgen Kiebach and set deco
 rator Herbert Strabel. Their work brought the film’s time and place to l
 ife and provided Fosse with a realistically claustrophobic Kit Kat Klub\, 
 which nonetheless made room for the filmmaker’s typically thrilling musi
 cal numbers.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/cabaret-019fce45-9a13
 -42b2-5717-e48163a64c67
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Thing in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261004T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261004T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcda7-1449-ab44-0006-af9e9afec03e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, October 4\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 49m\n\n1982 | 109 min | USA | Color |
  Scope | English | Rated R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: John Carpenter \nPROD
 UCED BY: David Foster\, Lawrence Turman \nWRITTEN BY: Bill Lancaster  \n
 WITH: Kurt Russell\, A. Wilford Brimley\, T. K. Carter\, David Clennon\n\n
 Type: Screenings\n\nIn the first installment of what Carpenter would later
  call his Apocalypse Trilogy\, a research team in Antarctica runs afoul of
  a shapeshifting alien menace. This bleak and stylish sci-fi horror thrill
 er features Kurt Russell as resourceful helicopter pilot MacReady and Bill
  Lancaster’s screenplay in a faithful adaptation of John W. Campbell’s
  1938 novella Who Goes There?\, which was first filmed in 1951 as The Thin
 g from Another World. Upon release\, Carpenter’s film was overlooked in 
 favor of Steven Spielberg’s record-breaking\, friendly alien adventure E
 .T. the Extra-Terrestrial (1982)\, but in the ensuing decades\, The Thing 
 has become a contemporary classic\, influencing many filmmakers. It was se
 lected for preservation in the National Film Registry by the Library of Co
 ngress in 2025.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-thing-019fcda7-14
 49-ab44-0006-af9e9afec03e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:There’s No Business Like Show Business
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261005T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261005T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce20-c52d-80ff-535a-88bc053ac702@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, October 5\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Muse
 um of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 57m\n\n1954 | 117 min | USA | Color |
  Scope | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Walter Lang \nPRODUCED 
 BY: Sol C. Siegel \nWRITTEN BY: Phoebe Ephron\, Henry Ephron \nWITH: Ethel
  Merman\, Donald O'Connor\, Marilyn Monroe\, Dan Dailey\n\nType: Screening
 s\n\nThe Donahue clan\, led by husband and wife Molly and Terry (Ethel Mer
 man and Dan Dailey)\, navigate the ups and downs of show biz\, from their 
 beginnings in Vaudeville to the Great Depression\, in this musical comedy 
 with songs by Irving Berlin. The family’s close bond is further tested w
 ith the arrival of the talented and driven Vicky Parker (Marilyn Monroe). 
 Costume designer Travilla played an integral role in shaping Marilyn Monro
 e’s public image on- and off-screen. Together they famously produced loo
 ks that evaded censorship while still courting controversy. His designs fo
 r her showgirl characters are of particular note.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/theres-no-business-li
 ke-show-business-019fce20-c52d-80ff-535a-88bc053ac702
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Resurrection in 4K with Ellen Burstyn
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261005T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261005T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce4f-6735-e9e2-7f81-f98161be92de@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:4K restoration world premiere \nIn person: Ellen Burstyn\n\nMo
 nday\, October 5\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion P
 ictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 43m\n\n1980 | 103 min. | USA | Color | English | Ra
 ted PG | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Daniel Petrie \nPRODUCED BY: Renée Missel
 \, Howard Rosenman \nWRITTEN BY: Lewis John Carlino \nWITH: Ellen Burstyn
 \, Sam Shepard\, Roberts Blossom\, Eva Le Gallienne\n\nType: Screenings\n
 \nAn ordinary woman survives a near-death experience and discovers newfoun
 d powers of healing in this supernaturally tinged drama\, directed by Dani
 el Petrie (A Raisin in the Sun\, 1961) from an original screenplay by Lewi
 s John Carlino (The Great Santini\, 1979) that emphasizes character and em
 otion over fantasy. Resurrection was a passion project for actress Ellen B
 urstyn\, who worked closely with Carlino to develop the screenplay\, recei
 ving a Best Actress nomination for her subtle\, heartfelt work. Theater le
 gend Eva Le Gallienne was also nominated for her thoughtful performance as
  Burstyn’s grandmother\, three-time Oscar winner Maurice Jarre (Lawrence
  of Arabia\, 1962) composed the soaring score\, and the striking visual ef
 fects for the afterlife sequence were created by Tony Silver and Oscar nom
 inees Richard Greenberg and Robert Greenberg (Predator\, 1987).
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/resurrection-with-ell
 en-burstyn-019fce4f-6735-e9e2-7f81-f98161be92de
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:There’s No Business Like Show Business
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261007T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce21-4b72-f70e-fd5c-ed37d22f368d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Wednesday\, October 7\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy M
 useum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 57m\n\n1954 | 117 min | USA | Colo
 r | Scope | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Walter Lang \nPRODUC
 ED BY: Sol C. Siegel \nWRITTEN BY: Phoebe Ephron\, Henry Ephron \nWITH: Et
 hel Merman\, Donald O'Connor\, Marilyn Monroe\, Dan Dailey\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nThe Donahue clan\, led by husband and wife Molly and Terry (Ethel 
 Merman and Dan Dailey)\, navigate the ups and downs of show biz\, from the
 ir beginnings in Vaudeville to the Great Depression\, in this musical come
 dy with songs by Irving Berlin. The family’s close bond is further teste
 d with the arrival of the talented and driven Vicky Parker (Marilyn Monroe
 ). Costume designer Travilla played an integral role in shaping Marilyn Mo
 nroe’s public image on- and off-screen. Together they famously produced 
 looks that evaded censorship while still courting controversy. His designs
  for her showgirl characters are of particular note.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
