BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Horror of Dracula in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261024T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261024T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce86-2243-5854-5276-e69b16a7e623@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:US 4K restoration premiere\n\nSaturday\, October 24\, 2026 at 
 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 22m
 \n\n1958 | 82 min. | UK | Color | English | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Terence 
 Fisher \nPRODUCED BY: Anthony Hinds \nWRITTEN BY: Jimmy Sangster \nWITH: P
 eter Cushing\, Christopher Lee\, Michael Gough\, Melissa Stribling\n\nType
 : Screenings\n\nThe stars (Peter Cushing\, Christopher Lee)\, screenwriter
  (Jimmy Sangster)\, and director (Terence Fisher) of Hammer Films’ 1957 
 breakout hit The Curse of Frankenstein reunited for this exciting adaptati
 on of Bram Stoker’s classic novel. Cushing brought subtlety and intellig
 ence to the character of Dr. Van Helsing\, which he would play four more t
 imes for Hammer\, and Lee’s quietly charismatic portrayal of the Count (
 a role he would reprise in six sequels) made him one of the most iconic in
 terpreters of the role. With its bold Technicolor cinematography by Jack A
 sher and the thunderous orchestral score by Hammer regular James Bernard\,
  the film helped establish the studio as a brand name in big-screen horror
 .
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/horror-of-dracula-019
 fce86-2243-5854-5276-e69b16a7e623
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Men in Black in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261025T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261025T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce46-c5ce-0889-bd76-0e3266b76f15@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, October 25\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 38m\n\n1997 | 98 min. | USA | Color | E
 nglish | Rated PG-13 | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Barry Sonnenfeld \nWRITTEN BY:
  Ed Solomon \nPRODUCED BY: Walter F. Parkes\, Laurie MacDonald \nWITH: Tom
 my Lee Jones\, Will Smith\, Linda Fiorentino\, Vincent D’Onofrio\nPrint 
 courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nA New York po
 lice officer is recruited into a secret government organization that helps
  hide the existence of extraterrestrial visitors from the public\, and tea
 ms up with a more experienced operative to track down a malicious alien th
 at has taken over a human host. Tommy Lee Jones and Will Smith make an une
 xpectedly ideal mismatched pair of heroes\, later reuniting for two sequel
 s\, and are supported by witty performances from Linda Fiorentino\, Rip To
 rn\, and a physical tour-de-force from Vincent D’Onofrio as the possesse
 d farmer. Rick Baker and David LeRoy Anderson won Oscars for their imagina
 tive alien makeup designs\; Danny Elfman was nominated for his lively scor
 e\; and production designer Bo Welch and set decorator Cheryl Carasik were
  nominated for the film’s art direction\, which deftly combined retro-fu
 turist sets and authentic New York locations.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/men-in-black-019fce46
 -c5ce-0889-bd76-0e3266b76f15
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Christine
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261025T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261025T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce1e-0dad-beb1-27fe-7e0bb544d83a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, October 25\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 50m\n\n1983 | 110 min | USA | Color 
 | Scope | English | Rated R \nDIRECTED BY: John Carpenter \nPRODUCED BY: R
 ichard Korbitz \nWRITTEN BY: Bill Phillips \nWITH: Keith Gordon\, John Sto
 ckwell\, Alexandra Paul\, Robert Prosky\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAdapted fro
 m Stephen King's 1983 novel\, John Carpenter's Christine follows bullied h
 igh schooler Arnie (Keith Gordon) and his growing attachment to his sentie
 nt car. As Arnie transforms into a revenge seeker\, blindly loyal Christin
 e abets Arnie in hunting down bullies and questioners of the bewildering r
 elationship. The '58 Plymouth Fury is also\, seemingly\, indestructible\, 
 reverting to mint condition whenever tarnished\, thanks to the work of Aca
 demy Award-nominated visual effects supervisor Roy Arbogast (Close Encount
 ers of the Third Kind\, 1977). This cult classic is the perfect blend of K
 ing's thriller and Carpenter's unique take on the supernatural.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/christine-019fce1e-0d
 ad-beb1-27fe-7e0bb544d83a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:They Live by Night in 35mm with Nathan Gelgud and A. S. Hamrah
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261026T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261026T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce87-a827-bf83-b8a9-0fc6ac7bc84d@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: cartoonist Nathan Gelgud and critic A. S. Hamrah\n
 \nMonday\, October 26\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Mot
 ion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 35m\n\n1948 | 95 min | USA | Black-and-white |
  English | Not Rated | 35mm  \nDIRECTED BY: Nicholas Ray  \nPRODUCED BY:
  John Houseman \nWRITTEN BY: Charles Schnee\, Nicholas Ray  \nWITH: Cath
 y O’Donnell\, Farley Granger\, Howard Da Silva\, Jay C. Flippen\n\nType:
  Screenings\n\nBefore Terrence Malick’s Badlands (1973)\, before Arthur 
 Penn’s Bonnie and Clyde (1967)\, there was Nicholas Ray’s They Live by
  Night (1948)\, a book-to-screen adaptation about young lovers on the run.
  After busting out of prison\, Bowie (Farley Granger) hides out at a filli
 ng station owned by a dad with alcoholism and his daughter Keechie (Cathy 
 O’Donnell) and starts knocking off banks with his fellow escapees. After
  a spending spree leaves Bowie injured and vulnerable to the law\, Keechie
  falls for him\, and the couple sets off an ill-fated journey\, hopeful fo
 r a new life.\nIn Ray’s remarkable debut feature\, classic noir elements
  blend effortlessly with melodrama to create something entirely new\, maki
 ng They Live by Night a standout of the period\, realized with lived-in di
 alogue beautifully adapted from Edward Anderson’s source material and sh
 ot primarily on location in greater Los Angeles. The film is notable too f
 or its innovative camera techniques and tracking shots\, especially in the
  driving sequences. This warm-up for Rebel Without a Cause (1955) finds Ra
 y arriving on the scene fully formed with a confident and inimitable style
 .  \nA favorite of cartoonist Nathan Gelgud and critic A. S. Hamrah\, the
  screening is followed by a conversation about Ray and his oft-overlooked 
 influence on American filmmaking. In a separately ticketed event before th
 e film\, Gelgud and Hamrah sign copies of their newest books.  \n \nProg
 rammed and note by K.J. Relth-Miller.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/they-live-by-night-wi
 th-a-s-hamrah-and-nathan-gelgud-019fce87-a827-bf83-b8a9-0fc6ac7bc84d
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Road to Perdition in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261028T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261028T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce2f-801e-c756-e852-6f258f23222f@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Sound Branch\n\nWednesday\, October 28\, 2026 
 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 5
 7m\n\n2002 | 117 min. | USA | Color | Scope | English | Rated R | 35mm \nD
 IRECTED BY: Sam Mendes \nPRODUCED BY: Richard D. Zanuck\, Dean Zanuck\, Sa
 m Mendes \nWRITTEN BY: David Self \nWITH: Tom Hanks\, Paul Newman\, Jude L
 aw\, Jennifer Jason Leigh\nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\n
 Type: Screenings\n\nDirector Sam Mendes’s next film after his 1999 Best 
 Picture winner American Beauty is a somber and gorgeously crafted gangster
  drama\, based on a graphic novel series by Max Allan Collins and Richard 
 Piers Rayner. Tom Hanks plays Michael Sullivan\, a loving father and stoic
  enforcer for an Irish-American crime family (led by supporting actor nomi
 nee Paul Newman)\, who must flee with his young son. Conrad L. Hall won a 
 posthumous Oscar for his dark\, moody cinematography\, and the film was al
 so nominated for its art direction (Dennis Gassner\, Nancy Haigh)\, Thomas
  Newman’s moving score\, and the atmospheric contributions of supervisin
 g sound editor Scott A. Hecker and sound mixers Scott Millan\, Bob Beemer 
 and John Patrick Pritchett.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/road-to-perdition-019
 fce2f-801e-c756-e852-6f258f23222f
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Decision to Leave (헤어질 결심) with Park Hae-il
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261029T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261029T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce27-6a63-4b4d-9352-4a2c3fd9026e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: actor Park Hae-il\n\nThursday\, October 29\, 2026 a
 t 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 18
 m\n\n2022 | 138 min | South Korea | Color | Scope | Korean | Not Rated | D
 CP \nDIRECTED BY: Park Chan-wook \nWRITTEN BY: Park Chan-wook\, Chung Seo-
 kyung \nPRODUCED BY: Park Chan-wook\, Ko Dae-seok \nWITH: Park Hae-il\, Ta
 ng Wei\, Lee Jung-hyun\, Park Yong-woo\n\nType: Screenings\n\nPark Hae-il 
 stars in Park Chan-wook’s Hitchcockian neo-noir thriller as Hae-joon\, a
  detective whose obsessions over his cases interfere with his daily life. 
 A man’s body is found at the foot of a cliff after what seems to be an a
 ccident\, until the detective encounters Seo-rae (Tang Wei)\, the wife of 
 the deceased. From there\, Hae-joon falls into a spiral of dichotomy\, str
 uggling between irresistible infatuation and reasonable suspicion against 
 her as a murder suspect. The chemistry between Park and Tang generates a g
 entle breeze of grounding emotional force\, tugging the audience into enig
 matic spaces where reality and dreams merge\, and the only consistent lang
 uage is love. Park’s portrayal of Hae-joon is executed in powerful ambig
 uity\, harmonizing beautifully with the director’s cinematic language.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/decision-to-leave-019
 fce27-6a63-4b4d-9352-4a2c3fd9026e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Hansan: Rising Dragon (한산: 용의 출현) with Park Hae-il
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261030T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261030T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce28-77e9-867e-b116-4fbf49e1a102@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: actor Park Hae-il\n\nFriday\, October 30\, 2026 at 
 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 10m
 \n\n2022 | 130 min | South Korea | Color | Scope | Korean/Japanese | DCP 
 \nDIRECTED BY: Kim Han-min \nWRITTEN BY: Kim Han-min\, Lee Na-ra\, Yun Hon
 g-gi \nPRODUCED BY: Kim Han-min\, Ahn Taewoon\, Shin Chang-hwan \nWITH: Pa
 rk Hae-il\, Byun Yo-han\, Ahn Sung-ki\, Son Hyeon-ju\n\nType: Screenings\n
 \nhe second installment of the epic Yi Sun-sin trilogy by director Kim Han
 -min stars Park Hae-il as the 48-year-old Yi Sun-sin\, an admiral of the J
 oseon dynasty (1392-1910) venerated for seemingly miraculous victories ove
 r the Japanese naval forces during the Imjin War period. The film focuses 
 on the 1592 Hansan Island battle against a fleet overseen by daimyo (feuda
 l lord) Wakisaka Yasuharu\, who plans to conquer the Joseon peninsula on a
  quest to devour the entire Ming Dynasty. Yi’s famously optimized Turtle
  battleship appears in grandiose\, precisely executed glory under Yi’s o
 rchestration. Spectacular visual compositions\, complemented by remarkable
  visual effects and a magnificent score\, come together to deliver one of 
 most gratifying naval war scenes ever shown on the Korean big screen.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/hansan-rising-dragon-
 019fce28-77e9-867e-b116-4fbf49e1a102
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Witches
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261031T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261031T121500
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce44-08a6-7d94-e7af-9bf92c6bcc07@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Accessible screening\n\nSaturday\, October 31\, 2026 at 11 am
 \n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 31m\n\n1990
  | 91 min. | United Kingdom\, USA | Color | English | Open Captions | Rate
 d PG | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Nicolas Roeg \nPRODUCED BY: Mark Shivas \nWRI
 TTEN BY: Allan Scott \nWITH: Anjelica Huston\, Mai Zetterling\, Bill Pate
 rson\, Jasen Fisher\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAccessible screeningOn the last
  Saturday of every month\, the museum's Family Matinee is an accessible sc
 reening where we offer open captioning\, keep the theater dimly lit\, and 
 maintain a lower volume for viewers who are neurodiverse. \nThey walk amon
 g us\, often appearing as ordinary women\, but when one notices those purp
 le eyes\, gloved hands\, and keen smell for children\, it can mean only on
 e thing: a witch. Luke\, an orphaned 9-year-old\, is vacationing in a seas
 ide hotel with his grandma. So is an undercover witch convention. After th
 e Grand High Witch turns Luke into a mouse\, he finds himself scurrying to
  save the rest of the world’s children from a similar fate. This wicked 
 tale\, adapted from Roald Dahl’s children’s novel of the same name\, f
 eatures the work of Jim Henson’s Creature Shop in the iconic and terrify
 ing reveal of the witches' true forms\, and an unforgettable performance b
 y Anjelica Huston as the Grand High Witch.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-witches-019fce44-
 08a6-7d94-e7af-9bf92c6bcc07
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Museum After Dark
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261031T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261031T224500
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019f240a-2c67-7918-db74-8932990ed413@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Celebrate Halloween at the Academy Museum with a goth-themed a
 fter-hours party: cocktails\, tarot readings\, a special guest\, and a scr
 eening of The Craft (1996).\n\nSaturday\, October 31\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLoc
 ation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nSchedule\n7–10pm | After-hou
 rs access to The Horror Show\n7–9:30pm | DJ\, bar\, and bites\n7–9:30p
 m | Tarot\, palm reading\, and astrology readings\n8pm | Inside Horror Aft
 er Dark: Thrilling Makeup Effects with Ken Diaz \n10pm | Screening: The Cr
 aft (1996) | David Geffen Theater\nThis event has early access for Academy
  Museum members.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nStep into the shadows and join the
  Academy Museum on Halloween for special after-hours party.\nGet into the 
 Halloween spirit with a goth-themed night of brews and bites\, tarot readi
 ng\, spellbinding music\, an anniversary screening of a beloved cult class
 ic\, a thrilling special guest\, and more.  \nInspired looks are highly en
 couraged. We want to see your hauntingly glamorous costumes. Adults 21 and
  over.  \nThis event has early access for Academy Museum members. \nNot a 
 member? Join now for early access to this program\, plus year-round discou
 nts and free general admission.  \n\nSchedule7–10pm | After-hours access
  to The Horror Show   7–9:30pm | DJ\, bar\, and bites  7–9:30pm | Taro
 t\, palm\, and astrology readings  Embrace your past\, present\, and futur
 e in a fun tarot card\, palm\, or astrology reading.   \n8pm | Inside Horr
 or After Dark: Thrilling Makeup Effects with Ken Diaz Venture behind the s
 cenes of your favorite horror films to learn about one of the genre’s mo
 st important disciplines. \nAcademy member and special effects makeup arti
 st Ken Diaz\, whose credits include The Thing (1982)\, My Bloody Valentine
  (2009) and Sinners (2025)\, gives a 30-minute talk deep within the harrow
 ing halls of our exhibition The Horror Show\, unveiling elements of bloody
  good makeup effects. \nStay for a brief Q&A with Diaz after the talk. \n1
 0pm | Movie | The Craft (1996) | David Geffen Theater
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/museum-after-dark-019
 f240a-2c67-7918-db74-8932990ed413
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:30th Anniversary of The Craft
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261031T220000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261031T234500
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce89-65f1-d75b-7550-d6d55b63dd80@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: actors Robin Tunney and Rachel True\n\nSaturday\, O
 ctober 31\, 2026 at 10 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n
 \nRuntime: 1h 41m\n\n1996 | 101 min | USA | Color | English\, French | Rat
 ed R | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Andrew Fleming \nPRODUCED BY: Douglas Wick \n
 WRITTEN BY: Peter Filardi\, Andrew Fleming \nWITH: Fairuza Balk\, Robin Tu
 nney\, Neve Campbell\, Rachel True\n\nType: Screenings\n\nSarah Bailey (Ro
 bin Tunney)\, a teen with a troubled past\, moves to Los Angeles with her 
 parents for a fresh start. Upon arrival in a Catholic school\, Sarah is in
 troduced to Bonnie (Neve Campbell)\, Rochelle (Rachel True)\, and Nancy (F
 airuza Balk)\, a clique of outsiders rumored to practice witchcraft. Born 
 a natural witch herself\, Sarah is welcomed to the group\, completing thei
 r coven. Together they invoke the ancient spirit of Manon\, a pagan deity.
  Critically panned upon release\, the film has since gained a cult followi
 ng for its fierce\, defiant portrayal of sisterhood\, female agency\, and 
 rage\, resonating with independent spirits\, outcasts\, and\, as Balk so p
 roudly puts it onscreen\, “the weirdos\, mister.” \nJoin us as we gath
 er after hours on All Hallows' Eve to commemorate 30 years since the relea
 se of a spellbinding classic.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/30th-anniversary-of-t
 he-craft-019fce89-65f1-d75b-7550-d6d55b63dd80
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gosford Park in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261101T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261101T153000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce7e-7fc4-7444-13d8-f903af546e07@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 1\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 17m\n\n2001 | 137 min. | USA/Germany/It
 aly/UK | Color | Scope | English | Rated R | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Robert Al
 tman \nWRITTEN BY: Julian Fellowes \nPRODUCED BY: Robert Altman\, Bob Bala
 ban\, David Levy \nWITH: Eileen Atkins\, Bob Balaban\, Charles Dance\, Ste
 phen Fry\nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings
 \n\nA weekend party at an English mansion in the 1930s leads to murder in 
 this seriocomic whodunit\, with Robert Altman directing an all-star cast. 
 The Oscar-winning screenplay came via Julian Fellowes\, who later explored
  the same milieu for Downton Abbey. The remarkable transatlantic cast incl
 udes Alan Bates\, Bob Balaban (who also produced and came up with the stor
 y idea with Altman)\, Clive Owen\, Kristin Scott Thomas\, Emily Watson\, a
 nd Helen Mirren and Maggie Smith\, who were both nominated for their perfo
 rmances. The film’s seven nominations include Best Picture\, Directing\,
  and Art Direction\, with production designer Stephen Altman and set decor
 ator Anna Pinnock mixing authentic English mansions and soundstage sets to
  evoke the glamour and class divisions of the era.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/gosford-park-019fce7e
 -7fc4-7444-13d8-f903af546e07
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Eungyo (은교) with Park Hae-il
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261101T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261101T193000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce81-14e6-a44b-f9a8-1dbc9ce699f2@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: actor Park Hae-il\n\nSunday\, November 1\, 2026 at 
 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 9m\n
 \n2012 | 129 min | South Korea | Color| Korean | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Jung J
 i-woo \nWRITTEN BY: Jung Ji-woo \nPRODUCED BY: Jung Ji-woo\, Kim Soon-ho 
 \nWITH: Park Hae-il\, Kim Go-eun\, Kim Mu-yeol\, Jeong Man-sik\n\nType: Sc
 reenings\n\nRenowned poet Lee Juk-yo\, 70\, has become a recluse\, mechani
 cally pushing through his bland days\, until they are pleasantly disrupted
  by high school student Eun-gyo\, who comes into his life\, bringing fresh
  energy. Lee starts working on a new novel\, the dullness of his life givi
 ng way to color\, possibilities\, even daydreams. But that color starts to
  dim when Seo Ji-woo\, Lee’s former student/assistant\, becomes involved
  with Eun-gyo\, and jealousy moves in instead. Based on a novel of the sam
 e name by Park Beom-shin\, Eungyo explores themes of youth\, and the poign
 ant notion of its loss and redemption\, with Park Hae-il delivering a nota
 ble interpretation of those themes through his character’s numbness and 
 dissociation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/eungyo-019fce81-14e6-
 a44b-f9a8-1dbc9ce699f2
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:F. W. Murnau’s Faust at 100 with a live score by Julianna Barwic
 k
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261101T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261101T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce83-299b-ab7d-c1f2-b65b04058a41@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 1\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum
  of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 47m\n\n1926 | 107 min | Germany | Black
 -and-white (tinted) | German intertitles with English subtitles | Not Rate
 d | DCP  \nDIRECTED BY: F. W. Murnau  \nPRODUCED BY: Erich Pommer  \nWR
 ITTEN BY: Gerhart Hauptmann\, Hans Kyser  \nWITH: Gösta Ekman\, Emil Jan
 nings\, Camilla Horn\, Frida Richard\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe haunting t
 ale of a scholar\, tempted by the Devil to sign away his soul in exchange 
 for universal knowledge\, has been a resonant cultural motif across five c
 enturies. If the essential literary text for the Faustian myth is Johan Wo
 lfgang von Goethe’s 19th century poetic drama\, its cinematic equivalent
  is F. W. Murnau’s visionary 1926 silent adaptation\, a masterpiece of h
 aunting visual design that explores rich themes of morality and materialis
 m. \nStarring Oscar-winning actor Emil Jannings as the terrifying trickste
 r Mephisto\, Murnau’s tale emerges from the disturbing underworld into t
 he dreams of lost innocence for one of the most remarkable films from the 
 German Expressionist milieu. A century later\, scores of international fil
 mmakers\, including Guillermo del Toro\, Jennifer Kent\, and Robert Eggers
 \, continue to be influenced by this masterwork. The Academy Museum is thr
 illed to present this film with live accompaniment on the occasion of its 
 centennial and of MUBI’s newest publication\, The Faust Bible: The Makin
 g of F. W. Murnau’s Masterpiece\, which will be available for purchase i
 n the Academy Museum Store.\nProgrammed and note by K.J. Relth-Miller.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/f-w-murnaus-faust-at-
 100-with-a-live-score-by-julianna-barwick-019fce83-299b-ab7d-c1f2-b65b0405
 8a41
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bus Stop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261102T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261102T153000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fce85-2ae5-dbb1-521b-f5d7bef18de9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, November 2\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mus
 eum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 36m\n\n1956 | 96 min | USA | Color |
  Scope | English | Not Rated | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Joshua Logan \nPRODUCED 
 BY: Buddy Adler \nWRITTEN BY: George Axelrod \nWITH: Marilyn Monroe\, Don 
 Murray\, Arthur O'Connell\, Betty Field\n\nType: Screenings\n\nThis contem
 porary Western—in which Marilyn Monroe stars as saloon singer Cherie\, w
 ho dreams of one day making it big in Hollywood—marked a turning point i
 n Marilyn Monroe’s career. She had recently formed Marilyn Monroe Produc
 tions and signed a new contract with 20th Century-Fox\, earning a higher s
 alary and more creative agency in the process. For the role of Cherie\, wh
 ich was one of the more dramatic and serious of her career to date\, she b
 egan working with Method acting pioneers Lee and Paula Strasberg\, who pla
 ced an emphasis on delving into an actor’s own experiences.
