BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:In Focus: Queer for Fear Live!
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261113T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261113T180000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fdd24-5d9c-9faa-8ac4-dd2a684b07a3@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Conversations
DESCRIPTION:Drag legend Peaches Christ and scholar Dr. Heather O. Petrocel
 li star in Queer for Fear Live!\, a show that blends scholarly analysis wi
 th stage performance.\n\nFriday\, November 13\, 2026 at 4 pm\n\nLocation: 
 Tea Room\, Level 5 (L5)\n\nType: Conversations\n\nQueer for Fear Live! ble
 nds scholarly analysis with stage performance in a mix of academic insight
 s\, personal anecdotes\, and the drag arts\, highlighting themes from the 
 book Queer for Fear: Horror Film and the Queer Spectator\, as well as the 
 new Horror Show exhibition at the Academy Museum. \nThe show is followed b
 y a meet and greet. \nAbout our guestsDrag legend Peaches Christ and schol
 ar Dr. Heather O. Petrocelli have created an engaging\, thought-provoking
 \, and entertaining experience that not only educates but also celebrates 
 queer culture and its love of horror.
LOCATION:Tea Room\, Level 5 (L5)
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/in-focus-queer-for-fe
 ar-live-019fdd24-5d9c-9faa-8ac4-dd2a684b07a3
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Black Fire with Kevin Jerome Everson and Claudrena N. Harold
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261113T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261113T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcea8-1372-4e4b-7da1-c6c524c438f6@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: filmmakers Kevin Jerome Everson and Claudrena N. Ha
 rold conversation\, moderated by filmmaker Cauleen Smith\n\nFriday\, Novem
 ber 13\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n
 \nRuntime: 20m\n\nFilms appear courtesy of the filmmakers and Picture Pala
 ce Pictures. All films directed by Kevin Jerome Everson and Claudrena N. H
 arold. \nSugarcoated Arsenic\n2013 | 20 min | USA | Black-and-white | Soun
 d | Not Rated | DCP\nPride\n2021 | 7 min | USA | Color | Sound | Not Rated
  | DCP\nAccidental Athlete\n2023 | 7 min | USA | Black-and-white | Sound |
  Not Rated | DCP\n70kg\n2017 | 2 min | USA | Black-and-white | Silent | No
 t Rated | DCP\nHow Can I Ever Be Late\n2017 | 5 min | USA | Black-and-whit
 e | Sound | Not Rated | DCP\nGospel Hill\n2022 | 5 min | USA | Black-and-w
 hite | Sound | Not Rated | DCP\nBlack Bus Stop\n2019 | 9 min | USA | Color
  | Sound | Not Rated | DCP\nTotal program runtime: approx. 57 min\n\nType:
  Screenings\n\nSince 2013\, artist Kevin Jerome Everson and author and his
 torian Claudrena N. Harold have cooperated on a series of lyrical short fi
 lms—13 to date—that explore Black intellectual\, social\, and politica
 l life at the University of Virginia in Charlottesville\, where both are p
 rofessors. Collectively titled Black Fire\, these works highlight the spac
 es where Black students have studied\, learned\, and organized through the
  years\, including the school's student-run newspaper and a bus stop that 
 became a locus for collective action. \nThrough short films spotlighting f
 igures such as Paulette Jones Morant\, one of the first Black woman schola
 stic athletes at UVA\, and Vivian Gordon\, the director of UVA’s Black S
 tudies program from 1974–1980\, Black Fire creates significant space for
  otherwise under-the-radar figures. Employing interviews and reenactment\,
  often casting their students as performers\, Everson and Harold's films b
 ring the history of UVA\, as well as the historical experiences of Black s
 tudents and faculty across the US\, into meaningful conversation with the 
 present day.  \n \nFilmmakers Kevin Jerome Everson and Claudrena N. Haro
 ld appear in conversation after the films\, moderated by filmmaker Cauleen
  Smith.  \n \nSpecial thanks to Madeleine Molyneaux\, Sam Raphael\, and 
 Now Instant Image Hall.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/black-fire-019fcea8-1
 372-4e4b-7da1-c6c524c438f6
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Frankenweenie in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T123000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcea8-e8f6-6d3e-76aa-b9ad54f74461@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, November 14\, 2026 at 11 am\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 27m\n\n2012 | 87 min. | USA\, Unite
 d Kingdom | Black-and-White | English | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Ti
 m Burton \nPRODUCED BY: Allison Abbate\, Tim Burton \nWRITTEN BY: John A
 ugust \nWITH: Catherine O'Hara\, Martin Short\, Martin Landau\, Charlie T
 ahan \nPrint courtesy of the Academy Film Archive\n\nType: Screenings\n\nB
 ased on Tim Burton’s 1984 short film\, Frankenweenie follows young Victo
 r Frankenstein as he goes about his childhood making short films with his 
 four-legged canine friend\, Sparky. When Sparky is killed by a car\, a dis
 traught Victor resurrects him. Soon other children start resurrecting thei
 r own pets\, and a horde of undead creatures wreaks havoc\, spurring the a
 dults to plot Sparky’s permanent demise. This black-and-white stop motio
 n Frankenstein adaptation features a score by frequent Burton collaborator
  Danny Elfman and is filled with homages to the classic horror films Burto
 n grew up loving\, resulting in a scary and sweet fairytale that will dazz
 le children and charm adults.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/frankenweenie-019fcea
 8-e8f6-6d3e-76aa-b9ad54f74461
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jealousy Is My Middle Name (질투는 나의 힘)  in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261114T200000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcea1-c7b8-b5d6-b554-26b3946dfe22@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, November 14\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy 
 Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n2002 | 125 min | South Kore
 a | Korean | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Park Chan-ok \nPRODUC
 ED BY: Kim Gwang-su\, Shin Chang-gil \nWITH: Park Hae-il\, Moon Sung-geun
 \, Bae Jong-ok\, Seo Young-hee \nPrint courtesy of Korean Film Archive.\n
 \nType: Screenings\n\nPark Hae-il plays Lee Won-sang\, the hero of Park Ch
 an-ok’s debut feature\, in his second big-screen appearance. Lee is a 27
 -year-old graduate student in literature\, finishing up his thesis before 
 he leaves for London. When he starts writing an article for a magazine as 
 a favor\, he realizes that the head editor\, Han Yun-shik\, is the reason 
 his girlfriend left him. Lee’s complicated feelings toward Han\, a mixtu
 re of admiration and jealousy\, brings more confusion when he is introduce
 d to a mesmerizing photographer\, Park Sung-yeon\, a cool\, down-to-earth 
 woman who is also having an affair with Han. Calm on the surface but roili
 ng underneath\, the story explores human emotions in a macro lens\, excava
 ting endless variables that shape characters while they struggle to stay c
 omposed. Park’s undefinable approach to Lee allows his character’s pur
 ity and honesty to roam free.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/jealousy-019fcea1-c7b
 8-b5d6-b554-26b3946dfe22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Grand Budapest Hotel in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261115T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261115T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcead-4761-2c05-b6ca-afca843ad169@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 15\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 39m\n\n2014 | 99 min. | USA/Germany | 
 Color | Scope | English\, French\, German | Rated R | 35mm\nDIRECTED/WRITT
 EN BY: Wes Anderson \nSTORY BY: Wes Anderson\, Hugo Guinness \nPRODUCED BY
 : Wes Anderson\, Scott Rudin\, Steven Rales\, Jeremy Dawson \nWITH: Ralph 
 Fiennes\, F. Murray Abraham\, Edward Norton\, Tony Revolori \nPrint courte
 sy of the Academy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\nWriter-director Wes
  Anderson was inspired by Austrian novelist Stefan Zweig (1881-1942) for h
 is seriocomic and visually dazzling story about a posh resort\, its vain c
 oncierge (Ralph Fiennes in a peerless comic performance) and a young lobby
  boy (Tony Revolori). Anderson directed an all-star international cast and
  brought his distinctive visual sensibilities to his original story (writt
 en with Hugo Guinness)\, set in a fictional European country on the eve of
  war. The film received nine nominations including Best Picture and won Os
 cars for Alexandre Desplat’s effervescent score\, Milena Canonero’s co
 stume design\, the makeup and hair creations of Frances Hannon and Mark Co
 ulier\, and the production design of Adam Stockhausen and his set decorato
 r\, Anna Pinnock\, who transformed a vacant German department store into t
 he eponymous hotel.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-grand-budapest-ho
 tel-019fcead-4761-2c05-b6ca-afca843ad169
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Paisàn in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261115T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261115T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceae-f096-eafc-82d4-f45ed5f0628e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 15\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 6m\n\n1946 | 126 min | Italy | Blac
 k-and-white | Italian/English/German/Sicilian/Latin/Hebrew with English su
 btitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Roberto Rossellini  \nPRODUCED
  BY: Rod E. Geiger\, Roberto Rossellini \nWRITTEN BY: Sergio Amidei\, Fede
 rico Fellini\, Roberto Rossellini\, Alfred Hayes\, Marcello Pagliero  \nW
 ITH: Carmela Sazio\, Robert Van Loon\, Benjamin Emanuel\, Raymond Campbell
 \n\nType: Screenings\n\nThe second installment in his WWII trilogy and one
  of Rossellini’s favorites of his films\, the episodic Paisàn is anothe
 r depiction of the tribulations of the Italians under German occupation. I
 ts title translates to “fellow countryman” and takes a broader view of
  Italy than his previous Rome\, Open City (1945)\, moving the action outsi
 de the capital to focus on the interactions between American armed forces 
 and the country residents impacted by conflict. Future Italian auteur Fede
 rico Fellini co-wrote the screenplay\, which came together on the fly\, wi
 th dialogue of each sequence written just before it was filmed.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/paisan-019fceae-f096-
 eafc-82d4-f45ed5f0628e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Germany Year Zero (Germania anno zero) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261115T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261115T203000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb0-6341-861a-27b3-b8a22f7f663a@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 15\, 2026 at 7 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 18m\n\n1948 | 78 min | Italy/France/Ge
 rmany | Black-and-white | German/English/French with English subtitles | N
 ot Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED/PRODUCED BY: Roberto Rossellini  \nWRITTEN BY
 : Roberto Rossellini\, Carlo Lizzani\, Max Kolpé \nWITH: Edmund Moeschke
 \, Ernst Pittschau\, Ingetraud Hinze\, Franz-Otto Krüger\n\nType: Screeni
 ngs\n\nReleased the same year the first theories of neo-realism were publi
 shed in film magazines Esprit\, La Revue du Cinéma\, and Bianco e Nero by
  acclaimed critics\, including André Bazin\, Germany Year Zero rounds out
  Rossellini’s WWII trilogy and brings a continental scope to the devasta
 ting impact of Hitler’s final solution. By centering the story on a youn
 g boy navigating the devastating implications of war\, Rossellini accentua
 tes the stark contrast between generations. A major influence on François
  Truffaut’s The 400 Blows (1959) and cast with people the director found
  by exploring the four zones of Berlin\, Germany Year Zero would\, Rossell
 ini hoped\, “reproduce the truth exactly as the camera saw it for that a
 udience through the entire world which has a heart capable of love and a b
 rain capable of thought.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/germany-year-zero-019
 fceb0-6341-861a-27b3-b8a22f7f663a
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Misfits in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261116T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261116T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb1-50e8-5b11-dffc-d44ae7c0f6e1@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, November 16\, 2026 at 2:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 5m\n\n1961 | 125 min | USA | Black-
 and-White | English | Not Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: John Huston \nPROD
 UCED BY: Frank E. Taylor \nWRITTEN BY: Arthur Miller \nWITH: Clark Gable\,
  Marilyn Monroe\, Montgomery Clift\, Thelma Ritter\n\nType: Screenings\n\n
 Penned by Arthur Miller\, this contemporary Western centers on the recentl
 y divorced Roslyn (Marilyn Monroe) and her relationship with an aging form
 er cowboy\, Gay (Clark Gable)\, who now survives by rounding up wild musta
 ngs to sell them to a slaughterhouse. Considered a commercial failure at t
 he time of its original release\, the film has since been regarded as a cl
 assic by critics and audiences\, perhaps notably because it was the final 
 completed film of both Gable and Monroe\, and a fitting bookend to Monroe
 ’s career—she credited Huston for her first big break in his The Aspha
 lt Jungle (1950).\nRestored by the Academy Film Archive and The Film Found
 ation. Restoration funding provided by the Hobson/Lucas Family Foundation.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-misfits-019fceb1-
 50e8-5b11-dffc-d44ae7c0f6e1
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:L’amore in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261116T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261116T203000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb4-23c7-f820-6cf6-bf395ffa1298@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, November 16\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 20m\n\n1948 | 80 min | Italy | Blac
 k-and-white | Italian | Not Rated | 35mm  \nDIRECTED/PRODUCED BY: Roberto
  Rossellini  \nWRITTEN BY: Roberto Rossellini\, Anna Benvenuti (“Una vo
 ce umana”)\; Federico Fellini\, Tullio Pinelli (“Il miracolo”) \nWI
 TH: Anna Magnani\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“I’ve made films in episodes b
 ecause I feel more at ease like that.” —Roberto Rossellini\, 1952 \nR
 ossellini’s appreciation for episodic storytelling\, which his later for
 ays into television allowed him to explore with more freedom\, first began
  in feature-length filmmaking. Part one\, titled “Una voce umana\,” is
  based on a play by Jean Cocteau and features the filmmaker’s then-lover
 \, esteemed actress Anna Magnani\, known only as the woman on the phone\, 
 speaking to a former flame. In part two\, “Il miracolo\,” a woman is s
 ure she is pregnant with the child of a saint. Dubbed the best foreign fil
 m that year by New York critics\, Amore was also met with derision when Ro
 ssellini was charged with blasphemy by the city’s archbishop due to the 
 content of the film’s second half.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/lamore-019fceb4-23c7-
 f820-6cf6-bf395ffa1298
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Getaway in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261118T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261118T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb5-074b-2299-31fc-5113d9814f93@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Selected by the Writers Branch \nFree for museum members\n\nWe
 dnesday\, November 18\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Mot
 ion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 2m\n\n1972 | 122 min. | USA | Color | Scope | 
 English | Rated PG | 35mm \nDIRECTED BY: Sam Peckinpah \nPRODUCED BY: Davi
 d Foster\, Mitchell Brower \nWRITTEN BY: Walter Hill \nWITH: Steve McQueen
 \, Ali MacGraw\, Ben Johnson\, Sally Struthers\nPrint courtesy of the Acad
 emy Film Archive.\n\nType: Screenings\n\n“Doc” McCoy (Steve McQueen) i
 s unexpectedly freed from a lengthy prison sentence to take part in a bank
  robbery\, but there may be no one he can trust\, not even his seemingly l
 oyal wife (Ali MacGraw). For one of his first produced screenplays\, Walte
 r Hill adapted one of noir master Jim Thompson’s darkest novels into a m
 ore light-hearted but still cynical romantic thriller\, with director Sam 
 Peckinpah staging the film’s action sequences with his usual vividness.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-getaway-019fceb5-
 074b-2299-31fc-5113d9814f93
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:50th Anniversary of Carrie with Sissy Spacek
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261119T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261119T213000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019efac4-788f-5c34-1b3d-6cf7811d1697@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:In person: Sissy Spacek\n\nThursday\, November 19\, 2026 at 7:
 30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 38m\n
 \n1976 | 98 min | USA | Color | English | Rated R | 4K DCP\nDIRECTED BY: B
 rian De Palma\nWRITTEN BY: Lawrence D. Cohen\nPRODUCED BY: Paul Monash\nWI
 TH: Sissy Spacek\, Piper Laurie\, Amy Irving\, William Katt\n\nType: Scree
 nings\n\nAdapted from the acclaimed debut novel by Stephen King\, Carrie f
 ollows a shy teen (Sissy Spacek) who gains telekinetic powers in late pube
 rty. Framing coming of age as a study in horror\, director Brian de Palma 
 subjects Carrie to abuse from classmates and her religious mother\, Margar
 et (Piper Laurie)\, culminating in one of the bloodiest prom scenes made f
 or the screen. The film garnered two Academy Awards nominations: Spacek ea
 rned her first acting nomination for her chilling\, subtle portrayal of a 
 vulnerable girl whose demeanor shifts with her newfound powers\, and Lauri
 e earned her second nomination (her first in a supporting role) for a dist
 ressing performance as a pious psychopath. Celebrate 50 years since the re
 lease of the indelible horror film with a screening in 4K and a conversati
 on with its star\, who went on to win an Oscar in Coal Miner’s Daughter 
 for Best Actress.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/carrie-with-sissy-spa
 cek-019efac4-788f-5c34-1b3d-6cf7811d1697
END:VEVENT
