BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Stromboli in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261121T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261121T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb7-6427-55b4-002f-045e576614b8@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, November 21\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy 
 Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 47m\n\n1950 | 107 min | Italy/USA
  | Black-and-white | English | Not Rated | 35mm  \nDIRECTED/PRODUCED BY: 
 Roberto Rossellini  \nWRITTEN BY: Roberto Rossellini \nWITH: Ingrid Berg
 man\, Mario Vitale\, Renzo Cesana\, Mario Sponzo\n\nType: Screenings\n\nNa
 med for the volcanic island in the Tyrrhenian Sea\, Rossellini's first col
 laboration with Swedish actress Ingrid Bergman is a heartbreaking tale abo
 ut a young Baltic woman who follows an Italian fisherman to his tiny\, iso
 lated village. Married swiftly after their release from an internment camp
 \, she begins to struggle as her refined upbringing clashes with the rough
  local lifestyle\, causing strain on her marriage and her psyche.  \nIt w
 as Bergman who first contacted Rossellini after seeing Paisàn in 1948 in 
 hopes of collaborating\, and the making of Stromboli marks the beginning o
 f a love affair that ended the filmmaker’s marriage and caused an intern
 ational scandal when Bergman announced their pregnancy. For Rossellini\, t
 he project also signals a creative shift\, at least to some critics\, away
  from realism and into a deeply psychological phase.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/stromboli-019fceb7-64
 27-55b4-002f-045e576614b8
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Favourite
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261122T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261122T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb8-441b-b5b9-ada8-4e32ba7253fa@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 22\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 59m\n\n2018 | 119 min. | UK/USA/Irelan
 d | Color | English | Rated R | DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Yorgos Lanthimos \nWRIT
 TEN BY: Deborah Davis\, Tony McNamara \nPRODUCED BY: Ceci Dempsey\, Ed Gui
 ney\, Lee Magiday\, Yorgos Lanthimos \nWITH: Olivia Colman\, Emma Stone\, 
 Rachel Weisz\, Nicholas Hoult\n\nType: Screenings\n\nOlivia Colman won the
  Best Actress Oscar for her richly detailed performance as England’s Que
 en Anne\, who finds two women competing for her affections in their quest 
 for power: the aristocratic Lady Sarah (supporting actress nominee Rachel 
 Weisz) and the impoverished Abigail Hill (supporting actress nominee Emma 
 Stone). Working from a darkly comic\, fact-based screenplay by Deborah Dav
 is and Tony McNamara\, director Yorgos Lanthimos (Poor Things\, 2023) subv
 erts the cliches of historical dramas to startling effect. The Favourite
 ’s 10 nominations include Best Picture\, Directing and Original Screenpl
 ay. Nominated production designer Fiona Crombie and set decorator Alice Fe
 lton used a stark\, black-and-white palate for their regal settings\, matc
 hing Sandy Powell’s elegant\, nominated costume designs.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-favourite-019fceb
 8-441b-b5b9-ada8-4e32ba7253fa
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Flowers of St. Francis (Francesco\, giullare di Dio) in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261122T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261122T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb9-32b4-32a5-5370-e7362e42607c@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 22\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 27m\n\n1950 | 87 min | Italy | Blac
 k-and-white | Italian | Not Rated | 35mm  \nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Roberto 
 Rossellini  \nPRODUCED BY: Angelo Rizzoli \nWITH: Aldo Fabrizi\, Gianfra
 nco Bellini\, Peparuolo\, Severino Pisacane\n\nType: Screenings\n\nInspire
 d by the writings of Saint Francis of Assisi and the hagiographic texts ab
 out his loyal follower Brother Juniper\, The Flowers of St. Francis is com
 posed of simple vignettes that beautifully illustrate the humane life less
 ons of the People’s Saint. Using only one professional actor\, with the 
 rest of the cast comprised of friars from the Franciscan monastery\, Rosse
 llini’s film brings into focus\, as he put it in 1950\, “the merrier a
 spect of the Franciscan experience\, the playfulness\, the ‘perfect deli
 ght\,’ the freedom that the spirit finds in poverty\, and in absolute de
 tachment from material things.”
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-flowers-of-st-fra
 ncis-019fceb9-32b4-32a5-5370-e7362e42607c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Machine That Kills Bad People (La macchina ammazzacattivi) in
  35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261123T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261123T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceb9-ef3b-238d-3db7-1f2c9aee90e9@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, November 23\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 23m\n\n1952 | 83 min | Italy | Blac
 k-and-white | Italian\, English | Not Rated | 35mm\nDIRECTED BY: Roberto R
 ossellini  \nPRODUCED BY: Salvo D’Angelo \nWRITTEN BY: Sergio Amidei\,
  Giancarlo Vigorelli\, Franco Brusati\, Liana Ferri\, Roberto Rossellini 
 \nWITH: Gennaro Pisano\, Marilyn Buferd\, William Tubbs\, Helen Tubbs\n\nT
 ype: Screenings\n\nOne of Roberto Rossellini's most singular and unheralde
 d works\, this potent blend of neorealism and fantasy is an idiosyncratic
 \, lighthearted fable. In a fishing village on the Amalfi Coast\, a strang
 er claiming to be a saint bestows a photographer’s camera with the power
  to kill bad people by photographing existing pictures of them. Initially
 \, the photographer uses his newfound weapon to eliminate abusers of power
 . But before long\, he succumbs to the very same amoral selfishness he onc
 e claimed to despise. When asked in a 1970 interview whether the film was 
 about filmmaking\, Rossellini replied\, "It's rather a game\, an attempt a
 t comedy\, [...] an isolated experiment."
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/the-machine-that-kill
 s-bad-people-019fceb9-ef3b-238d-3db7-1f2c9aee90e9
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Gremlins
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261128T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261128T123000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fceba-f20a-417b-ed3c-bd5163cf63fa@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Accessible screening\n\nSaturday\, November 28\, 2026 at 11 am
 \n\nLocation: Academy Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 46m\n\n1984
  | 106 min. | USA | Color | English | Open Captions | Rated PG | DCP \nDI
 RECTED BY: Joe Dante \nPRODUCED BY: Michael Finnell \nWRITTEN BY: Chris 
 Columbus \nWITH: Zach Galligan\, Phoebe Cates\, Hoyt Axton\, Polly Hollid
 ay\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAccessible screeningOn the last Saturday of ever
 y month\, the museum's Family Matinee is an accessible screening where we 
 offer open captioning\, keep the theater dimly lit\, and maintain a lower 
 volume for viewers who are neurodiverse. \nWhen Billy receives a mysteriou
 s creature called a mogwai\, affectionately named Gizmo\, from his father 
 for Christmas\, he must follow three rules: keep it away from sunlight\, d
 on’t get it wet\, and above all else\, never feed it after midnight. Whe
 n the creature accidentally gets wet and more mogwai spawn with much more 
 devious intentions than the docile Gizmo\, mayhem erupts. With the town’
 s adults out of their depth\, it is up to Billy and Gizmo to defeat these 
 menacing creatures. The violence in the PG-rated Gremlins was one of the r
 easons the Motion Picture Association later created the PG-13 rating\; the
  film is often cited as kick-starting a trend of small creature features i
 n the 1980s. \nPlease note: This film contains discussions about Santa Cla
 us that might not be appropriate for younger viewers.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/gremlins-019fceba-f20
 a-417b-ed3c-bd5163cf63fa
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Europe ’51
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261128T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261128T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcebb-d046-d251-bb9d-d238cf827fbb@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Saturday\, November 28\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy 
 Museum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 58m\n\n1952 | 118 min | Germany a
 nd Italy | Black-and-White | Italian and English with English subtitles | 
 Not Rated | DCP\nDIRECTED BY: Roberto Rossellini \nPRODUCED BY: Roberto Ro
 ssellini \nWRITTEN BY: Roberto Rossellini\, Sandro De Feo\, Mario Pannunzi
 o\, Ivo Perilli\, Brunello Rondi \nWITH: Ingrid Bergman\, Alexander Knox\,
  Ettore Giannini\, Giulietta Masina\n\nType: Screenings\n\nAccording to Ro
 berto Rossellini\, Europe ’51 is a retelling of his film The Flowers of 
 St. Francis (Francesco\, giullare di Dio\, 1950)\, from a female perspecti
 ve. Ingrid Bergman portrays a wealthy American socialite living in Rome\, 
 moving through grief and guilt over the death of her son. As she sets out 
 on a journey of newfound selflessness\, devoting her time and money to the
  less fortunate\, her husband and the community question her sanity.  \nW
 ith Rossellini often pushing his actors to improvise dialogue\, Ingrid wou
 ld receive her lines only minutes before rolling. She would often referenc
 e this time as a difficult challenge. Working with first-time actors\, wit
 h no written dialogue\, and all during a Roman heat wave while Ingrid was 
 pregnant with twins Isabella and Isotta\, the director and actress proved 
 their grit as a filmmaking duo.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/europe-51-019fcebb-d0
 46-d251-bb9d-d238cf827fbb
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Once Upon a Time… in Hollywood in 70mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261129T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261129T160000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcebd-2334-62f5-48c4-fadf0162246e@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 29\, 2026 at 2 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Museu
 m of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 2h 41m\n\n2019 | 161 min. | USA | Color |
  Scope | English | Rated R | 70mm\nDIRECTED/WRITTEN BY: Quentin Tarantino 
 \nPRODUCED BY: David Heyman\, Shannon McIntosh\, Quentin Tarantino \nWITH:
  Leonardo DiCaprio\, Brad Pitt\, Margot Robbie\, Al Pacino\n\nType: Screen
 ings\n\nQuentin Tarantino’s alternate history of the late-’60s film sc
 ene focuses on the intertwining lives of three figures: fading TV Western 
 star Rick Dalton (Best Actor nominee Leonardo DiCaprio)\, his stunt double
  and best friend Cliff Booth (Supporting Actor winner Brad Pitt) and the r
 eal-life starlet Sharon Tate (Margot Robbie)\, whose life is destined to c
 ross paths with Charles Manson and his followers. With its massive\, stell
 ar cast (including Margaret Qualley\, Timothy Olyphant\, Austin Butler\, D
 akota Fanning\, Bruce Dern\, Damian Lewis\, and Al Pacino) and evocative\,
  needle-drop score\, Once Upon a Time elegantly evokes a lost era of Holly
 wood. Nominated for 10 Oscars including Best Picture\, it won for producti
 on designer Barbara Ling and set decorator Nancy Haigh\, who incorporate r
 eal-life locations such as the Musso & Frank Grill and the Playboy Mansion
  while creating believable living spaces for the protagonists.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/once-upon-a-time-in-h
 ollywood-019fcebd-2334-62f5-48c4-fadf0162246e
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Fear (La Paura) in 4K
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261129T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261129T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcebe-17ab-0bb5-1708-5843377f4226@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Sunday\, November 29\, 2026 at 6:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 24m\n\n1954 | 84 min | Germany and 
 Italy | Color | Italian/English/German/Spanish with English subtitles | No
 t Rated | 4K DCP \nDIRECTED BY: Roberto Rossellini \nPRODUCED BY: Herman M
 illakowsky \nWRITTEN BY: Sergio Amidei\, Franz von Treuberg\n\nType: Scree
 nings\n\nThe wife of a prominent German scientist\, Irene (Ingrid Bergman)
  had been keeping her affair under wraps until her lover’s jealous ex di
 scovers their dark secret. Under a cloud of blackmail\, Irene begins to lo
 se control\, panicking over the impending death of her marriage. Roberto R
 ossellini’s adaptation of Stefan Zweig’s novella was seen as a transit
 ion from neorealism into noir filmmaking\, focusing on the psychological e
 lements that send characters and their relationships into disarray. Filmed
  four years into their marriage\, Fear (La Paura)\, is remembered as the f
 inal collaboration between Roberto Rossellini and Ingrid Bergman before th
 eir abrupt separation in 1957.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/fear-019fcebe-17ab-0b
 b5-1708-5843377f4226
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Joan of Arc at the Stake in 35mm
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261130T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261130T223000
DTSTAMP:20260902T123625Z
UID:019fcebe-b2fc-b261-ca38-3420c3634eed@academymuseum.org
CATEGORIES:Screenings
DESCRIPTION:Monday\, November 30\, 2026 at 7:30 pm\n\nLocation: Academy Mu
 seum of Motion Pictures\n\nRuntime: 1h 20m\n\n1954 | 80 min | Italy\, Fran
 ce | Color | Italian with English subtitles | Not Rated | 35mm \nDIRECTED
  BY: Roberto Rossellini  \nPRODUCED BY: Giorgio Criscuolo\, Franco France
 se \nWRITTEN BY: Paul Claudel\, Roberto Rossellini  \nWITH: Ingrid Bergm
 an\, Tullio Carminati\, Giacinto Prandelli\, Augusto Romani\n\nType: Scree
 nings\n\nIn this filmed version of Paul Claudel and Arthur Honegger’s or
 atorio\, or sung mystery play\, Rossellini’s wife\, Ingrid Bergman\, pla
 ys Saint Joan of Arc in the final minutes of her life. Shot in rich Gevaco
 lor\, a Belgian affiliate of German Agfacolor\, this surreal musical is th
 e fourth of five projects the couple made together. In a 1954 interview\, 
 Rossellini said of the project: “It’s a very strange film. I know it w
 ill be said that my involution has reached its maximum limit\, that I am d
 igging my own grave. It is not a filmed play\, it is pure cinema\, and I w
 ould even go as far as saying it is neorealism as I have always intended i
 t.”\nA commercial failure on its release\, this 35mm screening of an imp
 orted 35mm print from Italy is one of the rarest titles featured in this s
 eries.
LOCATION:Academy Museum of Motion Pictures
URL:https://www.academymuseum.org/en/programs/detail/joan-of-arc-at-the-st
 ake-019fcebe-b2fc-b261-ca38-3420c3634eed
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
